## ASP.NET Framework → ASP.NET Core (.NET 8) Migration

This code uses two **ASP.NET request termination** patterns that rely on `Thread.Abort` internally:

| Pattern | Framework Behavior | Core Replacement |
|---------|-------------------|-----------------|
| `Response.End()` | Aborts thread to stop response | Just return from the action |
| `Response.Redirect(url, true)` | Aborts thread after redirect | Return `RedirectResult` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Swallows expected abort | Remove entirely |

---

### Migrated Code (ASP.NET Core / .NET 8)

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();

        // In ASP.NET Core, return a result — no Response.End() needed.
        // The framework handles content disposition and response lifecycle.
        return File(
            System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data),
            "text/csv",
            "report.csv"
        );
    }

    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Redirect()
    {
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            return Redirect("/login");
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
        // No ThreadAbortException catch needed — redirects don't throw.
    }
}
```

---

### Key Differences & Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| **No `Response.End()` equivalent** | In Core, returning an `IActionResult` ends the response. Never write to `Response.Body` then try to "end" it — just return. |
| **`Response.Redirect` doesn't abort** | Core's redirect just sets a 302 header. Code after it **continues executing**. Always `return` the result. |
| **`ThreadAbortException` never thrown** | `Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on .NET 6+. Remove all catch blocks for it. |
| **Null-safety on `User.Identity`** | In Core, `User.Identity` can be null. Use null-conditional (`?.`) access. |
| **No Web Forms event model** | `btnExport_Click` handlers don't exist. Use `[HttpPost]` action methods with explicit routes. |

---

### If you need streaming large CSVs:

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> ExportLarge(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    // Use HttpContext.RequestAborted (or injected token) for cooperative cancellation
    Response.ContentType = "text/csv";
    Response.Headers.ContentDisposition = "attachment; filename=report.csv";

    await using var writer = new StreamWriter(Response.Body);
    await foreach (var row in GenerateRowsAsync(cancellationToken))
    {
        await writer.WriteLineAsync(row);
        cancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    }

    return new EmptyResult();
}
```

The `CancellationToken` parameter is automatically bound to `HttpContext.RequestAborted` — if the client disconnects, your loop stops cooperatively instead of relying on `Thread.Abort`.
